So the answer: The quote is a mnemonic/meme noting that certain Celsius temperatures have Fahrenheit equivalents with reversed digits: 16°C ≈ 61°F (60.8) and 28°C ≈ 82°F (82.4). These two pairs are exactly 12°C apart, hence "every 12°C, the digits are inverted." It's a fun coincidence, not a general rule — it doesn't work for most temperatures, and it's only approximate (60.8 vs 61, 82.4 vs 82). Also -40°C = -40°F is a trivial case.

Also note: the pattern doesn't continue: 40°C = 104°F, not 04°F. So it's just a coincidence for those two values.

